# Visual Capture — UI Work Lifecycle

> **Mandatory for ALL UI work — engineers, QA, and reviewers alike.** Any ticket
> that touches visible UI must include before screenshots at mobile + desktop
> minimum and after screenshots at all four project viewports before it can
> move to `in_review`. If the change involves animation or multi-step
> interaction, a video recording is also required. **No visual review = no close.**

This skill covers two workflows:

1. **Build → Look → Adjust → Look Again** (for engineers implementing UI):
   capture before, build, capture after, iterate until correct at all viewports.
2. **Visual Verification** (for QA, reviewers, and non-engineering agents):
   open the running app, capture proof, and report findings with the exact
   scratch path.

Targets: the running app at `localhost:3000`, Storybook at `localhost:6006`,
or any other URL.

---

## MANDATORY pre-capture gate — do this BEFORE any capture (both workflows)

This skill captures and validates pixels. It does **not** carry product design
judgement, and it does **not** know what this UI is supposed to look like. Both
of those live elsewhere, and you MUST pull them in first — whether you are
building UI or reviewing it:

1. **Load the project's design-guide skill.** Expect one to exist (e.g.
   `tizzi-design-guide`, or a `<project>-design-system` skill). If you have not
   already loaded it this session, load it now and review through its lens
   (visual hierarchy, design tokens, accessibility, calm / low-cognitive-load —
   whatever that project defines). If you genuinely cannot find a design-guide
   skill, say so to the user before continuing — do **not** invent design
   judgement of your own.
2. **Find the wireframe for the UI piece you are capturing.** Expect a wireframe
   to exist (commonly under `docs/wireframes/`). Review your captures against its
   intended states, information density, and breakpoint behaviour, and reference
   it in your findings. If **no** wireframe exists for this piece, STOP and flag
   it with the user — a missing wireframe is a gap to raise, not to improvise
   past.

Neither step is optional and neither is satisfied by a smoke test. A capture
without the design lens and the wireframe is just pixels nobody judged — which is
exactly how UI drifts while every automated check still "passes".

## What this skill can do (know these exist, and use them)

The bundled `playwright-screenshot` CLI gives every agent these capabilities — do
not hand-roll browser tooling that reinvents them:

| Capability                                     | How                                                                                                                   |
| ---------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Simultaneous multi-viewport capture**        | All five project viewports in parallel in one run (subset/override with `--viewports`).                               |
| **Storybook capture**                          | Point at an `iframe.html?id=<story-id>` URL (use `--wait-until=domcontentloaded`).                                     |
| **Scripted interaction prep**                  | `--script <file.mjs>` runs your flow (open a sheet, fill a form, switch a tab) before the shot.                        |
| **Live-interaction recording + contact sheet** | With `--script`, records WebM, extracts frames, and builds a labelled contact sheet (`--video-fps=60` for animation). |
| **Crop to a single component**                 | `--clip-to=<selector>` captures only that element's bounding box (precise); or size a tight viewport for crops.        |

Each is documented in detail below and in `playwright-screenshot --help`.

> **NOT for live third-party sites or authenticated external sessions.** This
> skill drives a fresh Playwright Chromium against URLs you control (your dev
> server, Storybook, a localhost preview). It does **not** reuse a user's
> existing logged-in browser session and it does **not** know how to attach to
> an already-running Chrome. For authenticated state of a third-party site
> (Woolworths cart, bank balance, ticket booking), use **`live-session-capture`**.

