## Project Viewports

The CLI's five canonical viewports — used for **all** captures. Aligned with
Tailwind v3 default breakpoints, plus one Safari-constrained sibling for the
overlays / sheets / drawers / modals class of UI.

| Name            | Width × Height | Tailwind range | Purpose                                                              |
| --------------- | -------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `mobile`        | 390 × 844      | below `sm`     | iPhone 14 logical viewport with no browser chrome (fantasy ideal)    |
| `mobile_safari` | 390 × 664      | below `sm`     | iPhone Safari with top + bottom browser chrome (real visible region) |
| `tablet_p`      | 768 × 1024     | `md`           | iPad portrait                                                        |
| `tablet_l`      | 1024 × 768     | `lg`           | iPad landscape                                                       |
| `desktop`       | 1280 × 900     | `xl`           | Standard laptop / desktop                                            |

`mobile` and `mobile_safari` share the same logical width. The height difference
is what surfaces bugs that hide behind the "no browser chrome" assumption — most
visibly: modal close buttons clipped under the Safari header, sticky footers
fighting the Safari toolbar, sheets that resolve at the bottom of `100vh` but
not `100svh`.

**`mobile_safari` is mandatory** for every ticket that introduces or modifies a
fixed-position, sticky-positioned, or full-screen overlay. That includes:
modals, dialogs, bottom sheets, drawers, action sheets, app nav bars, toasts,
banners, snackbars, and any other layer that pins to a viewport edge.

If a particular ticket needs a different width or exact component viewport,
override per-call: `--viewports=mobile=375,desktop=1440` or
`--viewports=mobile=390x40`.

For tiny component animations, prefer an exact component-sized viewport or tight
crop over a full phone/desktop frame. The WebM and contact sheet must make the
moving pixels easy to inspect.

