## Required workflow for UI tickets

```text
0. GATE        → MANDATORY: load the project design-guide skill + locate the
                  wireframe and compare against it (flag with the user if missing)
1. ALIGN       → restate target as "route + viewport rule + expected behavior"
2. SOURCE      → run a dedicated app server on a unique port for this ticket
3. PRE-WORK    → capture current state at mobile + desktop minimum (before)
4. BUILD       → implement the change
5. LOOK        → open the browser, visually inspect at all viewports (design-guide lens)
6. ADJUST      → fix what doesn't look right
7. LOOK AGAIN  → repeat 5–6 until the UI is correct at ALL viewports
8. TEST        → Playwright interaction test of the changed flow (use --script)
9. CAPTURE     → final state, all five viewports (after, only after data settles);
                  `mobile_safari` is non-negotiable for fixed/sticky/overlay UI
10. REPORT     → post findings + exact scratch path; publish selected artifacts
                  only when an attachment-capable workflow explicitly requires it
```

**Steps 3–5 are the critical feedback loop.** Tests passing is necessary but
NOT sufficient for UI work.

### Alignment and source lock (mandatory)

Before any capture, write a one-line check in your notes or issue comment:

- `Target: /route | Rule: breakpoint/behavior | Expectation: what must happen`

Then lock capture provenance to one server instance:

- Start a dedicated app server on a unique port from the active worktree
  (example `3101`).
- Capture evidence only against that exact base URL and port.
- Include the full tested URL in the review comment (for example
  `http://localhost:3101/dashboard`).

This prevents mixed-localhost evidence from different checkouts.

### For QA and reviewers

```text
0. GATE        → MANDATORY: load the project design-guide skill + locate the
                  wireframe for this UI piece (flag with the user if missing)
1. OPEN        → ensure the dev server is running
2. INSPECT     → run the CLI against the changed route at all five viewports;
                  reject UI tickets that omit `mobile_safari` when the change
                  introduces or modifies a fixed/sticky/overlay layer
3. CAPTURE     → review the resulting PNGs (read them with the image viewer),
                  judged through the design-guide lens and against the wireframe
4. ASSERT      → for any sheet/modal/drawer/overlay, verify the geometry
                  assertion (see "Sheet/modal geometry assertions" below)
5. REPORT      → post findings and the exact scratch path; local-only trackers need no upload
```

