## Step 5 — Video recording (for interaction-heavy changes)

For animations, transitions, drawers, modals, multi-step forms, or drag-and-drop,
add `--script` to record video. Default is mobile + desktop only — override
with `--viewports=...` if needed.

Do not apply animation precision rules to ordinary journey capture. Journey
capture proves states and outcomes, so it can use the default/lower frame
sampling and fewer contact sheets unless animation timing, easing, or
frame-by-frame motion is the review target.

```bash
CAPTURES="$("$AGENT_FS_BIN" scratch "ui-visual-capture-$ISSUE")"

node .agents/skills/ui-visual-capture/scripts/playwright-screenshot.mjs \
  http://localhost:3000/YOUR_ROUTE \
  "$CAPTURES/<capture-name>/" \
  --script "$CAPTURES/interactions/open-meal-edit.mjs" \
  --viewports=mobile,desktop
```

The CLI automatically produces the full capture package for each viewport:

| File              | Location                                                     |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Settled-state PNG | `<capture-dir>/mobile.png`                                   |
| Source WebM       | `<capture-dir>/mobile.webm`                                  |
| Extracted frames  | `<capture-dir>/frames/mobile-webm/frame-001.png` …           |
| Contact sheet     | `<capture-dir>/contact-sheets/mobile-webm-contact-sheet.png` |
| Capture manifest  | `<capture-dir>/manifests/capture-manifest.json`              |

Use `--no-video` to skip the WebM (and all derivative captures) when you only
want the post-interaction PNG.

### Animation-specific capture (60fps minimum)

Apply this subsection only when reviewing animation, microinteraction,
transition timing, easing, or frame-by-frame motion.

For animation evidence:

- extract frames at **at least 60fps** with `--video-fps=60`,
- capture real browser pixels from the WebM; do not reconstruct motion from DOM
  styles, sampled widths, screenshots, or synthetic renders,
- use the actual app route or Storybook iframe URL and record the full URL,
- set the viewport to the actual component area or a tight viewport/crop (for
  example `--viewports=mobile=390x40`),
- trigger the real state change after the initial story/component has visibly
  settled.

Example Storybook animation capture:

```bash
SCRATCH="$("$AGENT_FS_BIN" scratch onboardingprogress-animation)"

node .agents/skills/ui-visual-capture/scripts/playwright-screenshot.mjs \
  'http://localhost:6007/iframe.html?id=atoms-onboardingprogress--welcome&viewMode=story&args=totalStages%3A5%3BactiveStage%3A3&globals=viewport.value%3Aphone' \
  "$SCRATCH/actual-390x40-60fps/" \
  --script "$SCRATCH/active-stage-3-to-4.mjs" \
  --viewports=mobile=390x40 \
  --viewport-only \
  --wait-until=domcontentloaded \
  --video-fps=60 \
  --contact-sheet-columns=10 \
  --contact-sheet-thumb-width=390 \
  --attach-existing
```

### Animated evidence rules

GIF output is deprecated and must not be generated. GIFs distort colour
palettes, can misrepresent timing, and are larger than their source WebM.

The WebM is the source of truth. The contact sheet is the mandatory
reviewer-friendly inspection artifact. The manifest records validity.
For animation review, generate the contact sheet from 60fps frame extraction.
For journey review, lower sampling is acceptable when it still proves the
states and final outcome.

Evidence is only valid when:

- the WebM and contact sheet reach the intended final UI state,
- the final frame matches the settled-state PNG,
- the evidence does not stop at a spinner (unless loading is the review target),
- all files remain inside `<capture-dir>/`.

### Publishing animation artifacts (conditional)

For local-only trackers, retain the entire package in scratch and report its exact path; no
upload is required. If an attachment-capable workflow explicitly requires
publication, publish the contact sheet PNG and settled-state PNG instead of the
WebM. Retain the WebM locally inside the scratch capture directory as the source
of truth.

The review comment must include:

- the settled-state PNG,
- the WebM contact sheet PNG,
- the capture output directory path,
- the manifest path,
- whether the final frame matches the settled screenshot.

