## Storybook-specific: story URLs

When the target is a Storybook story, use the **iframe URL** (no sidebar/chrome):

| Pattern                                           | Use for                                |
| ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| `http://localhost:6006/iframe.html?id={story-id}` | Screenshots and video — bare component |
| `http://localhost:6006/?path=/story/{story-id}`   | Browsing only                          |

Story IDs use kebab-case: `{category}-{component}--{story-name}` (e.g.
`flows-quick-capture--closed-state`). Discover them by browsing Storybook or
grepping `*.stories.tsx` for `title` + story export names.

Use URL `args` and `globals` to set the starting state. If a prop-only state
change must be triggered for video, run an interaction script that emits
Storybook's args update after the iframe story is visible:

```javascript
export default async function ({ page }) {
  await page.waitForTimeout(500)

  await page.evaluate(() => {
    const events = window.__STORYBOOK_MODULE_CORE_EVENTS__
    const channel = window.__STORYBOOK_ADDONS_CHANNEL__
    const storyId =
      window.__STORYBOOK_PREVIEW__?.currentSelection?.storyId ??
      new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('id')

    channel.emit(events.UPDATE_STORY_ARGS, {
      storyId,
      updatedArgs: { activeStage: 4 },
    })
  })

  await page.waitForTimeout(1200)
}
```

Prefer real UI input when the story exposes controls inside the iframe. Use
`UPDATE_STORY_ARGS` when the state is only reachable through Storybook args.
Because Storybook dev servers often keep websocket connections open, use
`--wait-until=domcontentloaded` if `networkidle` delays or fails capture.

