## Output JSON — what the CLI prints

The CLI emits a single JSON object on stdout. Parse it to confirm captures
succeeded before reporting the result or entering an explicitly requested
publication branch.

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "outputDir": "$CAPTURES",
  "manifestPath": "$CAPTURES/manifests/capture-manifest.json",
  "captures": [
    { "viewport": "mobile", "status": "ok", "path": "/abs/.../mobile.png" },
    { "viewport": "mobile_safari", "status": "ok", "path": "/abs/.../mobile_safari.png" },
    { "viewport": "tablet_p", "status": "ok", "path": "/abs/.../tablet_p.png" },
    { "viewport": "tablet_l", "status": "ok", "path": "/abs/.../tablet_l.png" },
    {
      "viewport": "desktop",
      "status": "ok",
      "path": "/abs/.../desktop.png",
      "videoPath": "/abs/.../desktop.webm",
      "framesDir": "/abs/.../frames/desktop-webm",
      "contactSheetPath": "/abs/.../contact-sheets/desktop-webm-contact-sheet.png"
    }
  ]
}
```

Top-level `status` is one of `ok`, `partial` (some viewports failed), or
`error` (all failed). Exit codes: `0` ok, `1` partial/all-failed, `2` bad
arguments, `3` playwright not found, `4` server error (auto-start or port conflict).

